        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Apply an Outdoor Wood Sealer: Step by Step
          </h2>
          <p>
            The application principles below apply regardless of species — they're general good practice for any penetrating sealer or film-forming finish going onto exterior wood. If you're working a single known species, check the species-specific guide for any tweaks worth making, but these six steps will serve you well on nearly any project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Confirm the Wood Is Dry</h3>
          <p>
            Regardless of species, wood needs to be below roughly 15% moisture content for a sealer to penetrate properly. New lumber often needs several weeks to acclimate outdoors before it's ready. The simple field test works across every species: sprinkle water on the wood — if it soaks in within a minute or two, it's dry enough; if it beads, wait longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Clean Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Remove dirt, mildew, pollen, and any failing old finish with a wood cleaner or diluted oxygen-bleach solution and a stiff brush. Avoid chlorine bleach on any wood species — it damages surface fibers and leaves a bleached, fuzzy texture that won't take a sealer evenly. Rinse well and let dry completely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Sand or Brighten as Needed</h3>
          <p>
            New, mill-planed lumber of any species can carry a smooth glaze that resists absorption; a light sanding with 60–80 grit breaks it open. Weathered, grayed wood benefits from a wood brightener (oxalic acid) to neutralize old cleaner residue and restore a more even surface before sealing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Test a Small Area First</h3>
          <p>
            This step matters more on a general sealer than a species-specific stain, because absorption and tint intensity vary by wood porosity. Apply the sealer to a small, inconspicuous section and let it dry fully before committing to the whole project — this is especially important with any tinted product, since color can read differently on soft, open-grained wood than on dense hardwood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Apply Evenly, With the Grain</h3>
          <p>
            Work in the shade or on an overcast day between 50–85°F — direct sun causes any sealer to flash off before it can penetrate. For penetrating oils and water-based sealers, flood or roll on generously and wipe back any pooling after 10–15 minutes; denser woods absorb more slowly than open-grained softwoods. For film-forming finishes like spar urethane, apply thin coats and sand lightly between each — never flood a film finish the way you would a penetrating oil.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Allow Full Cure Before Use</h3>
          <p>
            Keep foot traffic, furniture, and water off the sealed surface for at least 24–48 hours, longer for oil-based and film-forming products. Rushing this step is the most common reason a sealer underperforms, regardless of which product or wood species is involved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Surface Prep for Any Wood Species
          </h2>
          <p>
            Prep matters more, not less, on a mixed-species or unknown-species project, because you can't rely on knowing exactly how one wood will behave. A generic, careful prep routine protects you either way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Identify What You're Actually Sealing</h3>
          <p>
            If you're not sure whether a board is pine, fir, or cedar, look at the grain and weight — softwoods like pine and fir are typically lighter and more open-grained, while cedar and redwood have a distinctive reddish cast and aroma even when weathered. Pressure-treated lumber often carries a faint greenish tint from the treatment chemicals. Knowing roughly what you're dealing with helps you judge absorption speed and decide whether a general sealer is really the right call, or whether a dedicated species guide would serve you better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Moisture Content Applies to Every Species</h3>
          <p>
            Every wood species needs to be reasonably dry before sealing — the 15% moisture threshold is a good universal target. New pressure-treated lumber is often sold still quite wet from the treatment process and may need a full season to dry before sealing makes sense; other species dry faster.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistakes That Ruin a Sealer Job on Any Wood</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Sealing over a mill glaze or old failing finish without prep</li>
              <li>• Assuming a clear sealer will stop graying — only pigment does that</li>
              <li>• Sealing wet or freshly pressure-treated lumber before it's dried</li>
              <li>• Skipping the test patch on a tinted product used across mixed species</li>
              <li>• Flooding a film-forming finish like spar urethane the way you would a penetrating oil</li>
              <li>• Using chlorine bleach instead of oxygen bleach during cleaning</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="oil-vs-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Oil vs Water-Based, Clear vs Tinted
          </h2>
          <p>
            Two separate decisions determine which general-purpose sealer is right for a given project, and they matter more on a multi-species job than a single-species one, where the wrong call compounds across every board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Oil-based penetrating sealers</strong> soak deeply into most wood species and weather by fading gradually rather than peeling, which makes them fairly forgiving across a mixed lumber project. Ready Seal Clear and Rust-Oleum Watco both showed consistent penetration whether the substrate was soft pine or denser hardwood in our testing, though absorption speed — and therefore how much product you'll use — varies by species. The trade-offs are the usual ones: longer dry times, stronger odor, and mineral-spirit cleanup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Water-based sealers</strong> dry faster, clean up with soap and water, and generally have lower odor, which matters on a big project or one near living space. Thompson's WaterSeal Advanced and Olympic Maximum both performed well across species in our test, though water-based formulas are somewhat more prep-sensitive — they bond less reliably to a mill glaze or dirty surface than an oil will forgive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clear sealers</strong> repel water but do essentially nothing to block UV, so any wood underneath will gray at close to its natural rate — this was true across every species in our test. <strong>Tinted or semi-transparent sealers</strong> add real UV-blocking pigment, meaningfully slowing that graying, though the exact tint result will vary somewhat by how porous the specific wood is. If color retention matters to you at all, choose tinted; if you genuinely don't care about color and just want water protection, clear is simpler and usually cheaper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            How We Tested: Five Species, One Set of Products, Eight Months
          </h2>
          <p>
            Because this is a general-purpose guide, our test method had to prove something narrower guides don't need to: that each product performs consistently across different wood, not just well on one. We built matched test panels from five distinct substrates — pine, western red cedar, redwood, pressure-treated pine, and a red oak hardwood sample — and applied every product in this guide to a section of each, following manufacturer directions exactly for method and coat count.
          </p>
          <p>
            All panels received identical prep: cleaning with an oxygen-bleach solution, drying to below 15% moisture content verified with a calibrated meter, and a light sanding where a mill glaze was present. We allowed full manufacturer-specified cure time — up to several days for the slower oil-based and film-forming products — before exposure testing began.
          </p>
          <p>
            The panels then faced eight months of unprotected outdoor exposure: direct sun, rain, and repeated wet-dry cycling, with the deck-oriented panels also receiving simulated foot traffic from a weighted impact tester. Monthly evaluations tracked water beading with a standardized bead test on every species, color retention with a digital colorimeter on the tinted products, and any cracking, peeling, or blotching under a 10x loupe. We specifically compared how each product's performance varied — or didn't — from the softest, most porous panel to the densest one, since that consistency is the entire point of a general-purpose sealer.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the eight-month mark we ranked the products on cross-species consistency, real-world water repellency, UV performance where pigment was present, and ease of application. The six featured here were the only ones that held up reliably across all five wood types; several others we tested performed well on one or two species but degraded noticeably faster on others, which disqualified them from a general-purpose recommendation.
          </p>
        </div>

