            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best all-purpose outdoor wood sealer?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For a sealer that works well across pine, cedar, redwood, pressure-treated lumber, and hardwoods without any species-specific tuning, Thompson's WaterSeal Advanced Natural Wood Protector is our top overall pick — it beaded water reliably across every species we tested and applies in a single easy coat. For furniture and trim that need a glossy, waterproof film, TotalBoat Spar Marine Varnish is the better call. If you know your specific wood species and want the best possible color and longevity, a species-specific guide will usually outperform a general sealer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a general sealer or a species-specific stain?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A general-purpose sealer is the right call when you're sealing multiple wood types on one project, when you want simple water protection without worrying much about color, or when you don't know or don't care what species you're working with. If you have a single, known species and you care about maximizing color retention and lifespan, a species-specific stain formulated for that wood's exact porosity and tannin content will almost always outperform a general sealer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between clear and semi-transparent wood sealer?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Clear sealers contain little to no pigment, so they repel water effectively but do very little to block UV — wood underneath will still gray over time. Semi-transparent (tinted) sealers add enough translucent pigment to meaningfully slow UV graying while still letting the wood grain show through. Tinted products retained noticeably more of the wood's natural color after eight months outdoors than any clear sealer in the same conditions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based or water-based sealer better for outdoor wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Oil-based penetrating sealers generally soak deeper into wood fiber, condition the grain, and weather by fading gradually rather than peeling, which suits a wide range of species well. Water-based sealers offer lower odor, faster dry times, and soap-and-water cleanup. Neither is universally better across every wood species — the right choice depends on the specific wood, the climate, and how much maintenance you're willing to do.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I reseal outdoor wood?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It depends heavily on the product and sun exposure. Budget clear sealers like Thompson's Clear Multi-Surface typically need a fresh coat every year on sun-exposed wood. Premium clear and tinted penetrating oils generally run one to two years. Film-forming finishes like spar urethane can last several years before recoating but require full sanding once they finally fail. A simple water-bead test tells you when it's time regardless of the product.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the same sealer on a deck, a fence, and furniture?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Generally yes for water protection, but the best choice differs by use. Horizontal surfaces like deck floors take more abrasion and direct sun, favoring a durable penetrating oil or tinted sealer. Vertical surfaces like fences and siding see less wear and can use lighter clear sealers effectively. Furniture and fine trim benefit most from a film-forming marine spar varnish, which produces a glossy, fully waterproof surface a penetrating sealer can't match on pieces that see standing water or heavy handling.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
